faq:
  - q: "What are the strongest job markets in California in 2026?"
    a: "California has three distinct mega-markets: (1) San Francisco Bay Area — the global center of the technology industry. Despite post-2022 tech layoffs, the Bay Area remains the highest concentration of software engineers, product managers, venture capitalists, and AI researchers in the world. Dominant employers: Google, Meta, Apple, Salesforce, Stripe, Airbnb, OpenAI, Anthropic, and thousands of VC-backed startups. Compensation is the highest in the country; cost of living is correspondingly high. (2) Los Angeles — entertainment, media, and a growing tech ecosystem. Dominant industries: film and television (Disney, Netflix, Warner Bros., Universal), digital media, gaming (Riot Games, Activision-Blizzard, EA), FinTech, and tech (Snap, SpaceX Hawthorne operations). LA is increasingly a tech hub beyond entertainment. (3) San Diego — biotech and defense. Dominant employers: Qualcomm, Illumina, Pfizer San Diego, Thermo Fisher, SAIC, Leidos, and dozens of biotech companies. San Diego biotech is one of the three strongest biotech clusters in the US alongside Boston and South San Francisco."
  - q: "How does California state income tax affect job offer comparisons?"
    a: "California has the highest state income tax in the US — 13.3% marginal rate at the highest bracket, with the 9.3% bracket starting at $66K (single filers) in 2026. This has significant implications for offer comparison: (1) A $200K role in San Francisco nets roughly $130–140K after federal and state taxes. A $180K role in a no-income-tax state like Texas or Florida nets roughly $135–140K after federal taxes only. The nominal difference disappears after taxes, particularly for high earners. (2) California taxes RSU income as ordinary income when shares vest — for engineers with large RSU packages at FAANG, state tax on vested RSUs alone can exceed $50–100K annually. (3) Prop 19 and property tax dynamics affect California housing differently than other states. High earners in California should model after-tax income carefully before accepting or rejecting offers on nominal salary alone."
  - q: "What should software engineers in the Bay Area know about the current job market?"
    a: "The Bay Area tech job market has corrected significantly from the 2021–22 peak but remains the strongest in the US for senior engineers. Key realities in 2026: (1) Headcount is lower but compensation is higher at senior levels — companies that laid off significant portions of their workforce are hiring selectively at senior and staff levels, with stronger compensation packages for fewer roles. (2) AI/ML experience commands a significant premium — roles touching LLMs, foundation models, or ML infrastructure are the most in-demand and most competitive roles in the market. (3) The application volume for posted roles has increased dramatically post-layoffs — cold applications have lower response rates than in 2021–22. Referrals and network-sourced applications are increasingly the primary sourcing channel. (4) Competing offers remain the strongest salary negotiation tool — the practice of generating two or three FAANG offers simultaneously to drive up total comp is still effective and expected."
  - q: "How is the Los Angeles job market different from San Francisco?"
    a: "Los Angeles is a fundamentally different job market from San Francisco: (1) Industry diversity — LA has tech, entertainment, gaming, finance, and healthcare in significant concentrations. The SF Bay Area is more heavily concentrated in tech specifically. (2) Networking culture — LA networking is more social and entertainment-adjacent; connections are built at events, dinners, and through personal introductions rather than through professional forums. (3) Compensation structure — entertainment industry compensation is structured differently from tech (guild minimums, backend participation for creative roles, agent-negotiated deals) and from standard corporate compensation. Tech roles in LA generally pay slightly below equivalent Bay Area roles. (4) Geography matters — LA is geographically dispersed. A role in Culver City is a fundamentally different commute than a role in Burbank or Playa Vista. Location within LA affects industry cluster access significantly."
  - q: "What do biotech and life science professionals need for job searching in San Diego?"
    a: "San Diego biotech hiring has specific characteristics: (1) The community is small and relationship-driven — San Diego's biotech cluster is tight-knit. Conference attendance (BIO International, local biotech events), alumni networks from UCSD, Salk, and Scripps, and professional societies are primary networking channels. (2) Compensation is below South San Francisco and Boston — San Diego biotech pays slightly less than comparable Bay Area biotech roles, partially offset by lower San Diego living costs relative to SF. (3) Defense tech is a parallel ecosystem — Qualcomm, SAIC, Leidos, Northrop Grumman San Diego, and dozens of smaller defense contractors operate in parallel to biotech in San Diego. Security clearances are valued in this market. (4) Biotech career transitions within San Diego — moving from bench research to clinical development, regulatory affairs, or business development in biotech requires specific positioning that generic career coaches don't provide."
  - q: "How do California non-competes affect job searching?"
    a: "California has some of the most employee-friendly non-compete laws in the US — California Business and Professions Code Section 16600 makes most non-compete agreements unenforceable. This has significant implications for California job searching: (1) California employers cannot legally enforce post-employment non-competes — candidates leaving California employers are generally free to join competitors, work in the same industry, or start competing businesses immediately. (2) Non-solicitation agreements may still be enforceable — while non-competes are void, agreements not to solicit customers or employees of the former employer have more legal standing. (3) If you're considering leaving a California employer for a role in another state, the new employer may attempt to enforce that state's non-compete law — consult an employment attorney. (4) Trade secret protections still apply — while you can take your skills and knowledge, taking proprietary documents, customer lists, or confidential data creates separate legal exposure independent of non-compete enforceability."
